Call it PISA — Pantheon Instruction Set Architecture. The design should be closer to RISC than to a giant ontology: a small orthogonal kernel, with richer concepts compiled downward into combinations of primitives.

1. Machine model

PISA operates on a typed world graph rather than conventional RAM.

W_t=(N,E,A)

where N are things/states, E are relationships, and A are attributes.

Every instruction performs:

W_{t+1}=OP(W_t,\ inputs,\ parameters)

and automatically produces:

\Delta_t=W_{t+1}-W_t

So the machine never merely computes. It also records what changed, why, and because of which operator.

That makes the Delta Observatory native to the architecture.

PISA uses typed SSA-style references:

Type	Meaning
ENT	entity/agent/object
STATE	condition of something
REL	relationship
SIG	observation/signal
FRAME	interpretive coordinate system
MODEL	predictive/causal representation
GOAL	desired state
CONSTRAINT	permitted/forbidden region
VALUE	scalar/vector quantity
PROB	probability/confidence
TIME	temporal coordinate
RESOURCE	finite capability/currency
DELTA	difference between states

Instruction syntax:

%result = OP %input1, %input2 {parameters}

Example:

%d = COMPARE %expected, %observed

