2. PISA-RISC core

I think the irreducible useful kernel is about 20 instructions.

Opcode	Fundamental meaning	Example
OBSERVE	acquire information	read speed, hear statement
PROJECT	view through a frame	world → protagonist’s view
COMPARE	compute difference	expected vs actual
BIND	establish relation	hero ↔ mission
UNBIND	remove relation	employee leaves team
TRANSFORM	change a thing/state	weak → strong
SPLIT	one → many	faction divides
MERGE	many → one	signals fuse
GATE	permit/block flow	locked door, secrecy
FILTER	selectively preserve	attention mechanism
ROUTE	choose destination/path	dialogue branch
SCALE	modify magnitude	amplify threat
HOLD	preserve invariant	identity survives change
SEQUENCE	impose ordering	A before B
TRIGGER	condition → event	alarm upon threshold
FEEDBACK	output becomes input	reputation loop
TARGET	specify desired state	agent wants X
SELECT	choose among possibilities	decision
ACT	cause an external effect	attack, speak, move
UPDATE	revise internal model	learn from result

Those are the machine instructions.

Almost everything else should be a pseudo-instruction lowered into them.

⸻

3. Rich operators become macros

This is where the Pantheon vocabulary plugs in.

For example:

REVEAL(x,a)

isn’t primitive.

It lowers approximately to:

%knowledge = BIND %agent, %x
%access    = GATE %knowledge {open}
%view      = PROJECT %x, %agent_frame

Likewise:

HIDE(x)

becomes a gating/projection operation.

MIRROR(x)

can lower to:

%projection = PROJECT %x, %mirror_frame
%delta      = COMPARE %x, %projection

AMPLIFY becomes SCALE.

DAMPEN becomes SCALE with 0<k<1.

INVERT becomes TRANSFORM under an inversion mapping.

RECURSE becomes a feedback loop:

%next = TRANSFORM %current, %rule
%d    = COMPARE %next, %target
%loop = FEEDBACK %next {until %d < epsilon}

Now many Pantheon concepts become assembly language rather than metaphors.

⸻

4. The crucial instruction: FRAME

I would resist making FRAME an opcode.

A frame should instead be a first-class data type.

That’s stronger.

Then the same operation can behave differently under different frames:

PROJECT(x,F_1)\neq PROJECT(x,F_2)

For example:

PROJECT war, %soldier_frame
PROJECT war, %politician_frame
PROJECT war, %historian_frame
PROJECT war, %child_frame

Same referent.

Different projection.

That gives us formal machinery for perspective without pretending perspective changes the underlying object.

⸻

5. HOLD is unusually important

Traditional computing doesn’t need much philosophical machinery for identity.

Cross-domain computation does.

Consider:

Replace every texture, character model, setting, and animation in a game, but keep the mechanic.

What survives?

We need an explicit invariant operator:

%mechanic = HOLD %system {
    invariant = traversal_dynamics
}

Then:

%newgame = TRANSFORM %game {
    textures,
    characters,
    setting,
    narrative
}

subject to:

HOLD %traversal_dynamics

That tells the compiler:

Change everything permitted except this.

This is also the Ship of Theseus instruction.

⸻

6. Constraint is data, not merely control flow

A constraint should also be first-class.

%c1 = CONSTRAINT {
    stamina <= 100
}
%c2 = CONSTRAINT {
    character_cannot_fly
}

Then operations execute inside:

\mathcal A(W,C)

—the action-space allowed by the world and its constraints.

Change the constraint and you change the possibility space.

That handles games naturally:

UNBIND player, gravity_constraint

Suddenly flying becomes possible.

But it works equally well organizationally:

UNBIND employee, approval_requirement

Suddenly autonomous action becomes possible.

Same structural instruction.

Different domain.

⸻

7. Agency emerges from five primitives

An agent doesn’t require a mystical special case.

A minimal agent loop is:

OBSERVE
\rightarrow
TARGET
\rightarrow
SELECT
\rightarrow
ACT
\rightarrow
UPDATE

More concretely:

%s      = OBSERVE %world
%goal   = TARGET %desired_state
%choice = SELECT %possible_actions {relative_to=%goal}
%world2 = ACT %choice
%delta  = COMPARE %world2, %expected
%model2 = UPDATE %model, %delta

Then repeat.

That is already enough for an NPC.

Add memory, uncertainty, competing goals, social models, and recursive prediction and the NPC becomes increasingly agentic.

⸻

8. Narrative assembly

Now watch the same ISA become a story.

Suppose we want:

A protagonist wants the throne, trusts an ally, discovers the ally betrayed him, changes strategy, and attacks.

PISA:

%g1 = TARGET protagonist, throne
%r1 = BIND protagonist, ally {
    relation=trust
}
%secret = GATE betrayal_information {
    closed
}
%event1 = ACT ally {
    betray protagonist
}
%secret2 = GATE betrayal_information {
    open
}
%knowledge = OBSERVE betrayal_information
%d = COMPARE %ally_model, %knowledge
%model2 = UPDATE %ally_model, %d
%r2 = UNBIND protagonist, ally {
    relation=trust
}
%strategy2 = SELECT %strategies {
    target=%g1
    model=%model2
}
ACT protagonist, %strategy2

That’s essentially a story circuit.

Its genre is nowhere specified.

Compile it into:

* Shakespearean tragedy
* Star Wars
* Pokémon
* corporate succession drama
* mafia story
* political thriller

and the structural machine can remain largely intact.

⸻

9. Gauge assembly

Now compile an instrument.

%velocity = OBSERVE vehicle.velocity
%normalized = TRANSFORM %velocity {
    domain=[0,200]
    range=[0,1]
}
%display = PROJECT %normalized, needle_angle
ACT dashboard_needle, %display

Swap the projection:

PROJECT %normalized, audio_pitch

and you’ve created an auditory speedometer.

Or:

PROJECT %normalized, haptic_resistance

Now the accelerator itself can communicate speed.

Underlying variable:

v

Representation:

R(v,F)

This means UI design becomes compilation.

⸻

10. Gameplay mechanic decompiler

Suppose we observe Assassin’s Creed-style traversal.

Instead of storing:

CLIMB

we decompile it.

%surface = OBSERVE environment
%affordances = FILTER %surface {
    traversable=true
}
%routes = ROUTE %affordances {
    target=%destination
}
%route = SELECT %routes {
    utility=max
}
%movement = SEQUENCE %route.nodes
ACT player, %movement
%result = OBSERVE player.position
UPDATE %movement_model,
    COMPARE %expected_position, %result

Now nothing requires the domain to involve masonry or climbing.

Compile the structure into conversation:

surface      → social situation
affordance   → conversational opening
route        → sequence of topics
destination  → desired social outcome
movement     → utterance/action

You have transferred the climbing mechanic into social navigation.

That’s exactly the sort of strange cross-domain transplantation we want.

⸻

11. Pantheon Intermediate Representation

The ISA and IR should be separate.

PISA defines legal operations.

PIR stores programs built from them.

A PIR graph might look conceptually like:

ENTITY protagonist
TARGET protagonist -> objective
OBSERVE environment -> perception
FILTER perception -> affordances
SELECT affordances -> action
ACT action -> consequence
OBSERVE consequence -> result
COMPARE predicted result -> result -> delta
UPDATE world_model <- delta
FEEDBACK world_model -> next cycle

Visually that becomes a graph.

Mathematically it becomes a transformation network.

Serialized, it becomes code.

Narratively, it becomes a story.

Interactively, it becomes a game mechanic.

Organizationally, it becomes a workflow.

That’s why the IR matters so much.

⸻

12. Compilers

Each domain needs two translators:

Decompiler_D:D\rightarrow PIR

and

Compiler_D:PIR\rightarrow D

So:

Game
\xrightarrow{decompile}
PIR
\xrightarrow{compile}
Story

or:

Biology
\rightarrow PIR
\rightarrow Organization

or:

Music
\rightarrow PIR
\rightarrow UI

or:

MilitaryDoctrine
\rightarrow PIR
\rightarrow BusinessProcess

Critically, you do not assert that the two domains are identical.

You transfer operators and then test whether their causal behavior survives.

That prevents analogy from becoming hallucination.

⸻

13. Every instruction gets provenance

I would bake this into the hardware specification.

Every generated node carries:

source
operator
frame
confidence
timestamp
dependencies
constraints
predicted_effect
observed_effect
delta

So when something fails we can ask:

Where\ did\ the\ abstraction\ break?

Was it:

Decompiler?

IR?

Domain\ mapping?

Hidden\ constraint?

Wrong\ causal\ assumption?

That converts failure into calibration data.

⸻

14. PISA’s basic algebra

The instruction set creates a surprisingly compact algebra:

Bind

creates structure.

Transform

creates change.

Gate

creates accessibility.

Route

creates direction.

Select

creates choice.

Hold

creates continuity.

Compare

creates information.

Feedback

creates adaptation.

Target

creates purpose.

Act

creates consequence.

That’s a pretty serious little machine.

You can already see why only a small instruction set may be necessary.

