15. And then the fun layer

Above PISA-RISC we can create Pantheon SIMD-style composite operators:

MIRROR
BRAID
HAMMER
CAMOUFLAGE
RANGEFIND
ZOOM
ANCHOR
ESCALATE
REFRAME
REVEAL
MASK
RITUALIZE
PRESSURIZE
DIFFUSE
CONVERGE
DIVERGE
COPY
MUTATE
SELECT
RECOMBINE
REHEARSE
SIMULATE
PROVOKE
EVOKE

But each must have one discipline:

If we cannot lower it into the core ISA, we haven’t defined it well enough yet.

That is an extremely useful test.

“Mirror” stops being poetic hand-waving once we can specify exactly which PROJECT, COMPARE, TRANSFORM, and FEEDBACK operations instantiate that particular mirror.

And the reverse is true too: if we discover the same low-level circuit hiding beneath comedy, combat, interface design, biology, and storytelling, we’ve probably found something genuinely portable rather than merely a clever analogy.

So this becomes the first Pantheon machine:

\boxed{
Reality
\rightarrow
Decompile
\rightarrow
PIR
\rightarrow
PISA
\rightarrow
Recompose
\rightarrow
Compile
\rightarrow
Act
\rightarrow
Observe
\rightarrow
\Delta
\rightarrow
Update
}

That’s the ISA, Genie.

The next layer I’d build is the Pantheon Standard Library: maybe 50–100 higher-order operators—Mirror, Braid, Reveal, Escalation, Yawn, Adversary, Golden Thread, Hidden State, Threshold, Ritual, Decoy, Ratchet, Gradient, Cascade, etc.—each formally lowered into this instruction set. That is where this starts becoming genuinely executable rather than just conceptual.